{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/separator-vs-motif-check.md", "content": "# Full-width separator versus salient interior motif

When a test grid has a complete horizontal/vertical line alongside a conspicuous patterned region, don't select a transform just because the motif is visually rich. Start from exact input/output diffs in every demo. Record separator coordinates and whether separator cells change, plus every changed isolated marker and unchanged lookalikes. Compare candidate rules that operate on the separator/bands against rules operating on the motif, and require a single candidate to explain all positive and negative cells before projecting to the test.

In this instance, a minimal demo changed a sparse marker and recolored the adjacent full-width row below a full-width divider; another recolored the row immediately above a full-width divider while preserving a lone marker. The test had a bottom full-width color-2 row and an unrelated dense color-1 patch. The initial guess recolored the row above the divider and copied the patch as if it were a template; it was rejected. Do not infer a color-8 band merely by mirroring the first demo's local row offset. Need more discriminating evidence to establish which band relative to divider is selected and what sparse markers indicate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/separator-vs-motif-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Seed-motif generator checkpoint after a wrong submission

A salient colored seed embedded in a noisy grid does not by itself imply a copy rule. Candidate classes can include translated shape occurrences, periodic/lattice replication, directional rays, or combinations. Before extrapolating, record the seed's exact coordinates and the complete input/output difference mask in every demo. Verify both positive and negative cases: why each changed cell is selected, and why visually similar unchanged cells are excluded. Check palettes and dimensions that vary across demos; distinguish the seed color, target/background color, and unrelated noise empirically rather than assuming one role from a single pair.

After an incorrect submission, discard the whole generator. Do not move a few cells, swap a presumed background color, or switch to a nearby geometric interpretation without replaying it against every demo. With a costly attempt budget, use remaining demos for explicit competing hypotheses and only submit after a full-mask validation. Preserve all cells outside the supported transformation and verify dimensions/row lengths.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md", "content": "# Consecutive demo requests without synthesis

In this instance, five demo requests were made in succession across turns, despite each new pair changing the evidence materially. The examples looked unlike each other locally, but their salient relation was a long straight divider/marker line in color 2 plus an object on one side. In demonstrations, the output added a parallel line of color 8 on the opposite side of the object/partition, preserving the original line and object. The test similarly has a vertical 2 line at column 14 and a compact 0 motif immediately to its left; this suggests completing a parallel 8 line at column 11. This is the kind of structural synthesis that should happen after each pair, not after exhausting several demos.

Operational lesson: after every returned demo, record divider orientation/location, side containing the object, output line orientation/location, and preserved cells. Compare candidate relations across all prior examples. Do not issue the next request unless a specific unresolved fork remains and a new pair could distinguish it. A repeated pattern of asking for demos without stating the evolving hypotheses spends budget without increasing useful evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-geometry-vs-template.md", "content": "# Sparse geometry versus motif copying

When a sparse grid contains a compact colored motif plus isolated same-color marks, the motif may cue a geometric operation rather than serve as a template to replicate. Record the exact motif anchor and every marker coordinate. Test whether aligned markers define endpoints/borders and whether intervening cells are completed; infer eligible orientation, endpoint class, and scope separately. Account for existing markers that remain unchanged. Compare this with literal template-occurrence predictions against every demo, including negative evidence, before acting. A varied second geometry can discriminate the candidate mechanisms; request it only when it could alter the prediction.
