{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md", "content": "# Cellwise mask validation across heterogeneous demos

When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/mixed-demo-integration-lesson.md", "content": "# Mixed-demo integration lesson

This instance began with a salient 7-shaped motif recolored to 9 in the first demo, then showed a second different local recoloring (1 to 5) and a third (5 to 9) while many unrelated shapes remained untouched. The initial test guess simply recolored selected 7-motif occurrences and was wrong. These pairs do not justify extrapolating one template-copy or ray rule from their surface similarities.

For heterogeneous examples, record each complete diff and negative evidence, then seek a shared structural relation that accounts for all pairs. Separate trigger/scope, spatial selection, eligible input value, output color, and preservation behavior. Test against every demo before spending another demo request or submitting. If a common rule is not yet evident, do not continue reflexive demo chains: name alternative hypotheses and the specific evidence another example could distinguish.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/mixed-demo-integration-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md", "content": "# Post-error fill scope review

This stream contained one demo in which gaps between aligned markers were colored, another where the same kind of candidate rule was not evident, an identity example featuring a closed rectangular outline, and a test with several tempting but structurally different boundary patterns. Two speculative fill submissions failed. The durable lesson is to separate (1) whether the geometry is aligned/enclosed, (2) what configuration triggers action, and (3) which exact cells are in scope. An identity example is strong negative evidence against indiscriminately filling every enclosure or same-row/column gap.

After a rejected fill, reconstruct all demo diffs and explicitly compare gap-filling, enclosure-filling, and identity-preservation hypotheses against every pair. Do not infer the correct mask from a prior session note; verify the current grid's boundaries, coordinates, and dimensions. Spend another demo only after naming competing hypotheses and the specific outcome that would distinguish them. Treat a previous session's noted correction as a candidate to verify, never as a substitute for current-instance evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-session-review.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous-demo session review

This task supplied four very different demonstrations (17x15, 9x8, 18x17, and 25x12) using unrelated palettes and visible structures, while the test was 25x19 with sparse 7 markers and long 8 borders. Several demos encoded broad spatial propagation from edge patterns and preservation of sparse anomalies; their surface forms differed substantially. The crucial durable lesson is to infer the shared coordinate/structural relation from complete input-output diffs before acting, rather than treating each returned pair as permission for another reflexive demo request. After every pair, check both transformed and unchanged regions, then make the next action only if specific competing rules remain.

In this interaction, four demo requests were issued consecutively without a substantive hypothesis update in the action turn, and the final submission extrapolated a propagation rule without showing that it fit every demo. This is a costly failure mode: demo calls consume budget, and a plausible test pattern does not validate a generator. Maintain a single ledger across all pairs, name the distinguishing evidence for any further request, and only submit after the generator and output mask pass cross-demo checks.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-session-review.md"}

